 Here, I'll convert the term with base 5 to the term with base 125 in order to have the same residual term on both sides of the equation.
$5^{x + 4} = \bf {5^{3x}} {/eq}
Now, take the logarithm of both sides to remove the residual exponent term.
{eq}\bf \log_5(5^{x + 4}) = \log_5(5^{3x}) {/eq}
By property of logarithm, the base and the inside of logarithmic term will cancel out.
Now, simplify the remaining terms.
$$x + 4 = 3x\\ x - 3x = -4\\ \bf{-2x = -4}\\ \bf{x = 2} {/eq}
Answer: By applying the power of 2 to both sides of the equation, we have solved for the residual x. The answer x=2 satisfies the given equation.

